

                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; W.COM -- copy a file from CP/M out to the HOST.
                ;
                ;       W <cpmfile> [hostfile] [B|T]
                ;       W <pattern> [B|T]               e.g.  W *.ASM     W B:*.*
                ;
                ; Part of the altairsim Host Bridge (src/boards/hostbridge.h,
                ; docs/boards/hostbridge.md). Assemble it INSIDE the machine:
                ;
                ;       ASM W                   -> W.HEX
                ;       LOAD W                  -> W.COM
                ;
                ; The name is one letter because it is typed constantly -- see the same
                ; paragraph at the top of R.ASM. W talks to the Host Bridge card at 0B0H
                ; and to nothing else.
                ;
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; EVERY DISK OPERATION IN THIS PROGRAM IS A BDOS CALL, AND THAT IS THE
                ; WHOLE DESIGN. No BIOS entry points, no IN or OUT to a disk controller, no
                ; assumptions about a DPB, a sector size, a skew table or which card the
                ; drive is on. The only ports this program touches are the bridge's own
                ; two. That is what lets the SAME W.COM run against an 88-DCDD 8" disk, an
                ; 88-MDS minidisk and an 8 MB image without being reassembled.
                ;
                ; AND BDOS CLOBBERS EVERY REGISTER (A,B,C,D,E,H,L -- only SP survives).
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; B OR T -- AND WITH NEITHER, THE EXTENSION DECIDES.
                ;
                ; CP/M stores whole 128-byte records and keeps NO BYTE COUNT anywhere, so a
                ; file's true length is simply not recorded. A text file marks its own end
                ; with a ^Z, by convention, and CP/M ITSELF PADS NOTHING: what follows that
                ; ^Z in the last record is whatever the program that wrote the file left in
                ; its buffer. Some fill the record with ^Z (our own R does). Some write one
                ; ^Z and leave the rest as it was. Coming back out to the host, none of it
                ; can be told from real data.
                ;
                ;   B            every byte of every record, exactly. A .COM comes out
                ;                byte-for-byte identical, and a text file comes out with
                ;                the rest of its last record on the end: the ^Z, and up to
                ;                126 bytes of whatever followed it.
                ;
                ;   T            stop at the first ^Z. A text file comes out clean, and a
                ;                binary file that happens to contain a 1AH byte comes out
                ;                TRUNCATED.
                ;
                ; WITH NO SWITCH, W LOOKS AT THE EXTENSION OF THE CP/M NAME (see PICKMD):
                ;
                ;   ASM MAC Z80 HEX PRN LST SYM INC SUB TXT     -> T, and W says so
                ;   anything else                               -> B
                ;
                ; THE LIST NAMES TEXT, NOT BINARY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT. A list of
                ; names that "are really binary" (.COM, .REL, .DAT...) truncates the file
                ; that is not on it: one 1AH and it is cut short. This list is the other way
                ; round. A file that is not on it is copied exactly, so the worst a missing
                ; name costs is the tail of one record. Each name on it is a source, listing
                ; or hex file, which a ^Z ends by definition.
                ;
                ; .BAS AND .DOC ARE LEFT OFF ON PURPOSE. MBASIC saves tokens unless it is
                ; told otherwise, and a word processor's file can hold a 1AH.
                ;
                ; A SWITCH ALWAYS WINS, and W prints "(text, by extension)" on each file
                ; where the extension made the choice -- so it is never a silent one.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                
 0100                   ORG     100H
                
                ;---- CP/M ------------------------------------------------------------
 0005 =         BDOS    EQU     0005H           ; ALWAYS here. Never call the BIOS directly.
 0080 =         TAIL    EQU     0080H           ; the command tail: length, then the text.
                                                ; The CCP has already FOLDED IT TO UPPER
                                                ; CASE -- we cannot see lower case, ever.
 005C =         TFCB    EQU     005CH           ; the FCB the CCP parsed for us. W CAN use
                                                ; this and R cannot, and the reason is the
                                                ; whole difference between the two programs:
                                                ; W's first argument IS an 8.3 CP/M name, so
                                                ; the CCP's 8.3 parse is exactly right for it
                                                ; -- INCLUDING its expansion of `*` into the
                                                ; `?`s that BDOS search wants. R's first
                                                ; argument is a HOST name, which is not 8.3
                                                ; and which that parse would destroy.
                
 0002 =         CONOUT  EQU     2               ; E = char
 0009 =         PRSTR   EQU     9               ; DE = string, '$'-terminated
 000F =         FOPEN   EQU     15
 0014 =         FREAD   EQU     20              ; read sequential
 0011 =         FSRCHF  EQU     17              ; search for first
 0012 =         FSRCHN  EQU     18              ; search for next
 001A =         FSETDMA EQU     26
                
 0080 =         RECLEN  EQU     128             ; a CP/M record. Everything is a multiple.
 0040 =         HNMAX   EQU     64              ; a host name may be a PATH, so it is not 8.3
 0020 =         IDMAX   EQU     32              ; room for the card's signature
 0040 =         MAXN    EQU     64              ; how many wildcard matches we will hold
 001A =         CPMEOF  EQU     1AH             ; ^Z
 000D =         CR      EQU     0DH
 000A =         LF      EQU     0AH
                
                ;---- The Host Bridge (see HB.INC -- ASM.COM has no INCLUDE) -----------
 00B0 =         HBCMD   EQU     0B0H            ; OUT = command   IN = status
 00B1 =         HBDATA  EQU     0B1H            ; data, both ways
                
 0010 =         HERR    EQU     10H             ; the last operation failed
                
 0000 =         CIDENT  EQU     0
 0002 =         CVOPENW EQU     2
 0003 =         CCLOSE  EQU     3
 0007 =         CERROR  EQU     7
 0008 =         CRESET  EQU     8
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; MAIN
                ;=======================================================================
 0100 31B10F    START:  LXI     SP,STACK
                
                ; RESET first -- ONE byte. This card has no undefined state to climb
                ; out of.
 0103 3E08              MVI     A,CRESET
 0105 D3B0              OUT     HBCMD
                
 0107 CD4303            CALL    IDENT           ; is there even a card there? (exits if not)
 010A CDA503            CALL    PARSE           ; the tail -> CNAME, HNAME, MODE
 010D CD7D02            CALL    INIFCB          ; the CCP's FCB at 5CH -> ours
                
 0110 3ABE0B            LDA     CNAME
 0113 B7                ORA     A
 0114 CA8205            JZ      USAGE           ; no arguments at all
                
 0117 CDA002            CALL    HASWLD          ; does the FCB name hold a '?' ...
 011A B7                ORA     A
 011B C22401            JNZ     MANY            ; ...yes: it is a pattern
 011E CD8B01            CALL    ONEF            ; ...no: just the one file
 0121 C30000            JMP     0               ; warm boot
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; MANY -- a wildcard. Copy every CP/M file that matches.
                ;=======================================================================
                ; WE COLLECT ALL THE NAMES FIRST, AND THEN COPY THEM. That is not the
                ; obvious shape -- R interleaves its enumeration with its transfers -- and
                ; the asymmetry is forced by CP/M, not chosen:
                ;
                ; BDOS SEARCH NEXT (18) HAS NO CURSOR OF ITS OWN. It resumes from state
                ; that BDOS keeps in its ONE shared work area, and ANY other file call --
                ; the OPEN and the READs we are about to do -- overwrites it. Search first,
                ; open, read, search next is a documented way to walk off the end of a
                ; directory into someone else's file. So: take the whole list while nothing
                ; else is touching BDOS, then do the work.
                ;
                ; The HOST bridge has no such problem, which is why R can interleave: its
                ; enumerator is a real cursor that a transfer does not disturb.
                ;
                ; MAXN names is the cap, and hitting it is an ERROR, not a silent stop --
                ; a backup that quietly copied the first 64 of your 200 files would be
                ; worse than one that refused.
                ;=======================================================================
 0124 3A7D0B    MANY:   LDA     HNAME
 0127 B7                ORA     A
 0128 C28D05            JNZ     EWILD           ; "W *.ASM FOO.TXT" cannot mean anything
                
 012B AF                XRA     A
 012C 32AB0C            STA     NCNT
 012F 21B10C            LXI     H,NAMES
 0132 22AD0C            SHLD    NPTR            ; where the next match gets stashed
 0135 22AF0C            SHLD    CPTR            ; ...and where the copy phase will read from
                
 0138 11D20A            LXI     D,BUF           ; the search results land in the DMA buffer
 013B 0E1A              MVI     C,FSETDMA
 013D CD0500            CALL    BDOS
                
 0140 11590B            LXI     D,FCB
 0143 0E11              MVI     C,FSRCHF
 0145 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
                
 0148 FEFF      MN1:    CPI     0FFH
 014A CA5E01            JZ      MN2             ; 0FFH = no (more) matches
 014D CDB502            CALL    STASH           ; A holds the directory code -- see STASH
 0150 DAAE05            JC      ETOOMNY
 0153 11590B            LXI     D,FCB
 0156 0E12              MVI     C,FSRCHN
 0158 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 015B C34801            JMP     MN1
                
 015E 3AAB0C    MN2:    LDA     NCNT
 0161 B7                ORA     A
 0162 CAA305            JZ      ENOMAT
                
                ; ---- and now the copying, with BDOS's search state safely irrelevant ----
 0165 AF                XRA     A
 0166 32AC0C            STA     DONE
                
 0169 3AAC0C    MN3:    LDA     DONE
 016C 47                MOV     B,A
 016D 3AAB0C            LDA     NCNT
 0170 B8                CMP     B
 0171 CA8801            JZ      EXIT            ; every match done
                
 0174 CDE902            CALL    SETFCB          ; the next stashed name -> FCB
 0177 AF                XRA     A
 0178 327D0B            STA     HNAME           ; a FRESH host name is derived for each file:
                                                ; the previous one is still sitting there
 017B CD8B01            CALL    ONEF            ; (it reports its own failures and carries on)
                
 017E 3AAC0C            LDA     DONE
 0181 3C                INR     A
 0182 32AC0C            STA     DONE
 0185 C36901            JMP     MN3
                
 0188 C30000    EXIT:   JMP     0               ; warm boot
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; ONEF -- copy the CP/M file described by FCB out to the host.
                ;
                ; The FCB is already built -- by INIFCB from the command line, or by SETFCB
                ; from a directory entry. HNAME is the host name, or empty to derive one.
                ;=======================================================================
 018B CD4202    ONEF:   CALL    PICKMD          ; B or T for THIS file -- a wildcard asks again
                                                ; for each match
 018E 210000            LXI     H,0
 0191 22540B            SHLD    RECS
                
                ; A FRESH FCB FOR EVERY OPEN. Extent, the two system bytes, the record
                ; count and the current record must all be zero, and after a wildcard's
                ; second file the FCB we are handed is the one BDOS left behind.
 0194 AF                XRA     A
 0195 32650B            STA     FCB+12          ; ex
 0198 32660B            STA     FCB+13          ; s1
 019B 32670B            STA     FCB+14          ; s2
 019E 32680B            STA     FCB+15          ; rc
 01A1 32790B            STA     FCB+32          ; cr -- the sequential record cursor
                
                ; OPEN THE CP/M FILE FIRST, BEFORE TOUCHING THE HOST.
                ;
                ; The order is load-bearing, exactly as it is in R (for the mirror-image
                ; reason). OPEN_WRITE on the card truncates an existing host file, so
                ; creating it first would mean that `W TYPPO.TXT` -- a name that is not on
                ; the disk -- DESTROYS the host file of the same name and leaves an empty
                ; one behind. Fail first, destroy nothing.
 01A4 11590B            LXI     D,FCB
 01A7 0E0F              MVI     C,FOPEN
 01A9 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 01AC FEFF              CPI     0FFH
 01AE CAB905            JZ      ENOCPM
                
 01B1 AF                XRA     A
 01B2 32790B            STA     FCB+32          ; BDOS OPEN does not promise to clear cr
                
 01B5 3A7D0B            LDA     HNAME
 01B8 B7                ORA     A
 01B9 C2BF01            JNZ     OF1             ; the user said what to call it
 01BC CD1103            CALL    FCB2HN          ; ...or we name it after the CP/M file
                OF1:
                
                ;---- Open the host file ----------------------------------------------
 01BF 3E02              MVI     A,CVOPENW
 01C1 D3B0              OUT     HBCMD
 01C3 217D0B            LXI     H,HNAME
 01C6 CD7703            CALL    SENDZ           ; the NUL is what commits it
                
 01C9 DBB0              IN      HBCMD
 01CB E610              ANI     HERR
 01CD C2EE01            JNZ     ONEBAD          ; read-only card, outside the sandbox, bad name
                
 01D0 CD4F04            CALL    SAYXFR          ; "W: FOO.ASM -> FOO.ASM"
                
 01D3 11D20A            LXI     D,BUF
 01D6 0E1A              MVI     C,FSETDMA
 01D8 CD0500            CALL    BDOS            ; where BDOS puts each record it reads
                
 01DB CDF201            CALL    COPY            ; <-- the actual transfer
 01DE D8                RC                      ; carry = it failed and has already said so
                
                ;---- CLOSE IS WHAT COMMITS THE HOST FILE ------------------------------
                ; Not politeness: the card holds the whole file in memory and writes it in
                ; one go here. An unclosed write is DISCARDED -- which is precisely why a
                ; program that dies half way through a transfer cannot leave a truncated
                ; file on your host disk.
 01DF 3E03              MVI     A,CCLOSE
 01E1 D3B0              OUT     HBCMD
 01E3 DBB0              IN      HBCMD
 01E5 E610              ANI     HERR
 01E7 C2EE01            JNZ     ONEBAD          ; the host disk is full, or is read-only
                
 01EA CD7F04            CALL    SAYREC          ; "  3 records"
 01ED C9                RET
                
 01EE CD8003    ONEBAD: CALL    REPERR
 01F1 C9                RET
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; COPY -- CP/M records -> the host stream. Returns CY on failure.
                ;
                ; NO POLLING LOOP ON TBE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. The card is
                ; accumulating the file in host memory, not pushing it down a wire: it can
                ; always take the next byte, and a wait loop would be waiting for something
                ; that has already happened.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 01F2 210000    COPY:   LXI     H,0
 01F5 22540B            SHLD    RECS
 01F8 22560B            SHLD    BYTES           ; the TRUE byte count too -- 24 bits, because in T
 01FB AF                XRA     A               ; mode we stop mid-record and RECS*128 would lie
 01FC 32580B            STA     BYTES+2
                
 01FF 11590B    CP0:    LXI     D,FCB
 0202 0E14              MVI     C,FREAD
 0204 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 0207 B7                ORA     A
 0208 C23B02            JNZ     CPEND           ; 1 = end of file. Anything else is also an
                                                ; end, and there is nothing useful to say
                                                ; about it that CP/M has not already said.
                
 020B 2A540B            LHLD    RECS            ; count it now: in text mode we may stop half
 020E 23                INX     H               ; way through this record, and it was still
 020F 22540B            SHLD    RECS            ; read
                
 0212 21D20A            LXI     H,BUF
 0215 0680              MVI     B,RECLEN
                
 0217 7E        CP1:    MOV     A,M
 0218 4F                MOV     C,A             ; stash the byte -- LDA is about to eat A
 0219 3A520B            LDA     XMODE
 021C FE54              CPI     'T'
 021E 79                MOV     A,C             ; ...and give it back
 021F C22702            JNZ     CP2             ; binary: send everything, always
 0222 FE1A              CPI     CPMEOF
 0224 CA3B02            JZ      CPEND           ; text: the FIRST ^Z ends the file
                
 0227 D3B1      CP2:    OUT     HBDATA
 0229 CD9804            CALL    INCBYT          ; one more byte actually sent to the host -- count it
 022C 23                INX     H
 022D 05                DCR     B
 022E C21702            JNZ     CP1
                
                ; ASK ONCE PER RECORD, NOT ONCE PER BYTE. The only way a write can fail
                ; while it is still streaming is the card's 8 MB size cap, and a CP/M
                ; volume cannot be pushed past that in less than 65,536 records. Checking
                ; here costs one IN per 128 bytes and catches it long before we have
                ; shovelled a whole disk into a buffer that is never going to be written.
 0231 DBB0              IN      HBCMD
 0233 E610              ANI     HERR
 0235 C23D02            JNZ     CPERR
 0238 C3FF01            JMP     CP0
                
 023B AF        CPEND:  XRA     A               ; ...which also CLEARS CARRY: success
 023C C9                RET
                
 023D CD8003    CPERR:  CALL    REPERR
 0240 37                STC
 0241 C9                RET
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; PICKMD -- set XMODE, the mode for the file the FCB names.
                ;
                ; A switch on the command line settles it. With no switch, the extension in
                ; the FCB is looked up in EXTTAB: on the list is T, and AUTO is set so
                ; SAYXFR can say so; off the list is B.
                ;
                ; THE ATTRIBUTE BITS ARE MASKED OFF. CP/M keeps R/O, SYSTEM and ARCHIVE in
                ; the high bit of the three extension characters, so a read-only FOO.ASM
                ; has an 0C1H where its 'A' should be. STASH strips them for a wildcard;
                ; the FCB the CCP built for a single file never had them. The mask costs
                ; one instruction and means this routine does not care which it was given.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 0242 AF        PICKMD: XRA     A
 0243 32530B            STA     AUTO
 0246 3AD905            LDA     MODSET
 0249 B7                ORA     A
 024A 3AD805            LDA     MODE            ; (LDA leaves the flags alone)
 024D C27902            JNZ     PM9             ; the user said: that is the end of it
                
 0250 11DA05            LXI     D,EXTTAB
 0253 1A        PM1:    LDAX    D
 0254 B7                ORA     A
 0255 3E42              MVI     A,'B'           ; (so does MVI)
 0257 CA7902            JZ      PM9             ; off the end of the table: every byte
                
 025A 21620B            LXI     H,FCB+9         ; the three extension characters
 025D 0603              MVI     B,3
 025F 0E00              MVI     C,0             ; C collects every bit that differs
 0261 1A        PM2:    LDAX    D
 0262 AE                XRA     M
 0263 E67F              ANI     7FH
 0265 B1                ORA     C
 0266 4F                MOV     C,A
 0267 23                INX     H
 0268 13                INX     D
 0269 05                DCR     B
 026A C26102            JNZ     PM2             ; all three, always: DE must end on the next
                                                ; entry whether this one matched or not
 026D 79                MOV     A,C
 026E B7                ORA     A
 026F C25302            JNZ     PM1
                
 0272 3E01              MVI     A,1
 0274 32530B            STA     AUTO
 0277 3E54              MVI     A,'T'
 0279 32520B    PM9:    STA     XMODE
 027C C9                RET
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; THE CP/M DIRECTORY
                ;=======================================================================
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; INIFCB -- the CCP's parsed FCB at 005CH -> our own FCB.
                ;
                ; We copy it rather than use it in place. The CCP put a SECOND FCB at
                ; 006CH, twelve bytes in, and the moment BDOS opens the first one it writes
                ; an extent and a record count straight through the second. Copying is one
                ; loop and it makes the overlap somebody else's problem.
                ;
                ; Bytes 0..11 are all we want: the drive, and the eleven characters of the
                ; 8.3 name -- with `*` already expanded to `?` by the CCP, which is exactly
                ; the form BDOS SEARCH wants. The rest of the FCB must start at zero.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 027D 21590B    INIFCB: LXI     H,FCB
 0280 0624              MVI     B,36
 0282 3600      IF0:    MVI     M,0
 0284 23                INX     H
 0285 05                DCR     B
 0286 C28202            JNZ     IF0
                
 0289 215C00            LXI     H,TFCB
 028C 11590B            LXI     D,FCB
 028F 060C              MVI     B,12
 0291 7E        IF1:    MOV     A,M
 0292 12                STAX    D
 0293 23                INX     H
 0294 13                INX     D
 0295 05                DCR     B
 0296 C29102            JNZ     IF1
                
 0299 3A590B            LDA     FCB             ; remember the drive: a wildcard rebuilds the
 029C 32F905            STA     DRIVE           ; FCB for each match and must put it back
 029F C9                RET
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; HASWLD -- A=1 if the FCB name holds a '?', else A=0.
                ;
                ; We look at the FCB, not at the text, because the CCP has already done the
                ; expansion: it turned `*.ASM` into `????????ASM` before we ever ran. So
                ; `?` is the only wildcard character that still exists by the time we look.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 02A0 215A0B    HASWLD: LXI     H,FCB+1
 02A3 060B              MVI     B,11
 02A5 7E        HW1:    MOV     A,M
 02A6 FE3F              CPI     '?'
 02A8 CAB202            JZ      HWYES
 02AB 23                INX     H
 02AC 05                DCR     B
 02AD C2A502            JNZ     HW1
 02B0 AF                XRA     A
 02B1 C9                RET
 02B2 3E01      HWYES:  MVI     A,1
 02B4 C9                RET
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; STASH -- copy the name of the directory entry BDOS just found.
                ;
                ; BDOS SEARCH returns 0..3 in A: the entry is at BUF + A*32. (It hands back
                ; the whole 128-byte sector and tells you which of the four 32-byte entries
                ; in it was the hit. It does not hand you the entry.)
                ;
                ; Returns CY if the table is full.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 02B5 4F        STASH:  MOV     C,A             ; the directory code, before anything eats it
                
 02B6 3AAB0C            LDA     NCNT
 02B9 FE40              CPI     MAXN
 02BB D2E702            JNC     STFULL
 02BE 3C                INR     A
 02BF 32AB0C            STA     NCNT
                
 02C2 79                MOV     A,C
 02C3 E603              ANI     3               ; belt and braces: it is documented as 0..3
 02C5 87                ADD     A               ; *2
 02C6 87                ADD     A               ; *4
 02C7 87                ADD     A               ; *8
 02C8 87                ADD     A               ; *16
 02C9 87                ADD     A               ; *32 -- at most 96, so it stays in 8 bits
 02CA 5F                MOV     E,A
 02CB 1600              MVI     D,0
 02CD 21D20A            LXI     H,BUF
 02D0 19                DAD     D
 02D1 23                INX     H               ; -> the eleven name characters (past the
                                                ; user-number byte at offset 0)
 02D2 EB                XCHG                    ; DE = source
 02D3 2AAD0C            LHLD    NPTR            ; HL = where it goes
 02D6 060B              MVI     B,11
 02D8 1A        ST1:    LDAX    D
 02D9 E67F              ANI     7FH             ; STRIP THE ATTRIBUTE BITS. CP/M keeps R/O,
                                                ; SYSTEM and ARCHIVE in the HIGH BIT of three
                                                ; of the name characters, so a read-only
                                                ; FOO.COM has an 0C3H where its 'M' should be.
                                                ; Hand that to OPEN unmasked and it does not
                                                ; find the file it just told you about.
 02DB 77                MOV     M,A
 02DC 13                INX     D
 02DD 23                INX     H
 02DE 05                DCR     B
 02DF C2D802            JNZ     ST1
 02E2 22AD0C            SHLD    NPTR
 02E5 B7                ORA     A               ; ORA also CLEARS CARRY -- that is the point
 02E6 C9                RET
                
 02E7 37        STFULL: STC
 02E8 C9                RET
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; SETFCB -- the next stashed name -> FCB, and advance the cursor.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 02E9 21590B    SETFCB: LXI     H,FCB
 02EC 0624              MVI     B,36
 02EE 3600      SF0:    MVI     M,0
 02F0 23                INX     H
 02F1 05                DCR     B
 02F2 C2EE02            JNZ     SF0
                
 02F5 3AF905            LDA     DRIVE           ; the drive the user asked for. The directory
 02F8 32590B            STA     FCB             ; entry does not carry one -- it is a fact
                                                ; about the disk it was found on, and the
                                                ; entry has a USER NUMBER in that byte instead
                
 02FB 2AAF0C            LHLD    CPTR
 02FE EB                XCHG                    ; DE = the stashed name
 02FF 215A0B            LXI     H,FCB+1
 0302 060B              MVI     B,11
 0304 1A        SF1:    LDAX    D
 0305 77                MOV     M,A
 0306 13                INX     D
 0307 23                INX     H
 0308 05                DCR     B
 0309 C20403            JNZ     SF1
 030C EB                XCHG                    ; HL = one past the name...
 030D 22AF0C            SHLD    CPTR            ; ...which is the next one
 0310 C9                RET
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; FCB2HN -- name the host file after the CP/M one: FOO.ASM -> "FOO.ASM".
                ;
                ; The FCB pads both fields with SPACES, and no CP/M name may contain one,
                ; so a blank simply ends the field.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 0311 215A0B    FCB2HN: LXI     H,FCB+1
 0314 117D0B            LXI     D,HNAME
 0317 0608              MVI     B,8
 0319 CD3503            CALL    FLD
                
 031C 3A620B            LDA     FCB+9
 031F E67F              ANI     7FH             ; an attribute bit, not a letter -- see FLD
 0321 FE20              CPI     ' '
 0323 CA3203            JZ      F2Z             ; no extension: no dot either
 0326 3E2E              MVI     A,'.'
 0328 12                STAX    D
 0329 13                INX     D
 032A 21620B            LXI     H,FCB+9
 032D 0603              MVI     B,3
 032F CD3503            CALL    FLD
                
 0332 AF        F2Z:    XRA     A
 0333 12                STAX    D               ; NUL-terminated: that is what SENDZ wants
 0334 C9                RET
                
                ; Copy up to B characters from (HL) to (DE), stopping at the first blank.
                ;
                ; STRIP THE ATTRIBUTE BITS HERE TOO. STASH cleaned the name it put in the
                ; FCB, but this runs AFTER the OPEN -- and CP/M 3's OPEN writes the file's
                ; R/O, SYSTEM and ARCHIVE bits back into the high bit of the FCB's name
                ; characters. A read-only HELP.COM came back as HELP.0C3H'OM', which is
                ; no name a host will create.
 0335 7E        FLD:    MOV     A,M
 0336 E67F              ANI     7FH
 0338 FE20              CPI     ' '
 033A C8                RZ
 033B 12                STAX    D
 033C 13                INX     D
 033D 23                INX     H
 033E 05                DCR     B
 033F C23503            JNZ     FLD
 0342 C9                RET
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; THE HOST BRIDGE
                ;=======================================================================
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; IDENT -- prove there is a card there before we do anything else.
                ;
                ; THIS IS NOT CEREMONY. An IN from a port that no card decodes FLOATS THE
                ; BUS and the 8080 reads 0FFH. W's failure mode without this is quieter
                ; than R's and nastier: every OUT would go into the void, every status read
                ; would come back 0FFH -- which has HERR set -- and the program would
                ; announce a host error for a card that is not there. "Permission denied"
                ; is a much worse thing to tell someone than "there is no bridge".
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 0343 3E00      IDENT:  MVI     A,CIDENT
 0345 D3B0              OUT     HBCMD
                
 0347 21810C            LXI     H,IDBUF
 034A 0620              MVI     B,IDMAX
 034C DBB1      ID1:    IN      HBDATA
 034E 77                MOV     M,A
 034F B7                ORA     A
 0350 CA5A03            JZ      ID2             ; got the terminator
 0353 23                INX     H
 0354 05                DCR     B
 0355 C24C03            JNZ     ID1
 0358 3600              MVI     M,0             ; never terminated -- 0FFH forever, i.e. no card
                
 035A 21810C    ID2:    LXI     H,IDBUF
 035D 11C305            LXI     D,IDWANT
 0360 1A        ID3:    LDAX    D
 0361 B7                ORA     A
 0362 C8                RZ                      ; matched to the end of what we wanted: ours.
                                                ; A PREFIX compare, so a later card that says
                                                ; "... 2" still works with this .COM
 0363 BE                CMP     M
 0364 C26C03            JNZ     IDBAD
 0367 23                INX     H
 0368 13                INX     D
 0369 C36003            JMP     ID3
                
 036C 11FA05    IDBAD:  LXI     D,MNOHB
 036F 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 0371 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 0374 C30000            JMP     0               ; nothing else in this program can work
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; SENDZ -- send the NUL-terminated string at HL to the data port.
                ;
                ; The NUL goes too, AND IT IS THE NUL THAT COMMITS THE COMMAND. Until it
                ; lands the card has created nothing, so we could still change our mind by
                ; writing a different command byte.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 0377 7E        SENDZ:  MOV     A,M
 0378 D3B1              OUT     HBDATA
 037A B7                ORA     A
 037B C8                RZ                      ; that was the terminator: done
 037C 23                INX     H
 037D C37703            JMP     SENDZ
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; REPERR -- print what actually went wrong.
                ;
                ; The card hands back a code byte and then a MESSAGE in the host's own
                ; words ("FOO.TXT: permission denied"). We print the message and drop the
                ; code: a number the user has to go and look up is not an error report.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 0380 3E07      REPERR: MVI     A,CERROR
 0382 D3B0              OUT     HBCMD
 0384 DBB1              IN      HBDATA          ; the code byte -- read it and discard it
                
 0386 117F09            LXI     D,MERR
 0389 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 038B CD0500            CALL    BDOS
                
 038E DBB1      RPE1:   IN      HBDATA          ; ...then the text, a character at a time.
 0390 B7                ORA     A               ; Nothing is live across the BDOS call below,
 0391 CA9D03            JZ      RPE2            ; because the next byte comes from the PORT.
 0394 5F                MOV     E,A
 0395 0E02              MVI     C,CONOUT
 0397 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 039A C38E03            JMP     RPE1
                
 039D 118309    RPE2:   LXI     D,MCRLF
 03A0 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 03A2 C30500            JMP     BDOS            ; tail call: BDOS returns to OUR caller
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; THE COMMAND TAIL
                ;=======================================================================
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; PARSE -- pull up to three words out of the command tail at 0080H.
                ;
                ;       W FOO.TXT                 CNAME=FOO.TXT
                ;       W FOO.TXT T               CNAME=FOO.TXT              MODE=T
                ;       W *.* B                   a wildcard takes the switch too
                ;       W FOO.TXT BAR.TXT         CNAME=FOO.TXT HNAME=BAR.TXT
                ;       W FOO.TXT SRC/BAR.TXT T   ...and a subdirectory of hostdir
                ;
                ; A LONE `B` OR `T` IN THE SECOND SLOT IS THE SWITCH, NOT A HOST NAME, and
                ; that is an ambiguity we are choosing rather than one we are missing: a
                ; host file really called `T` exists somewhere. Write it as `./T` and the
                ; card drops the `.` component -- so the escape hatch costs two characters
                ; and no one has to learn a new syntax.
                ;
                ; The FCB comes from the CCP (see TFCB), so CNAME here is only ever used to
                ; answer "did the user type anything at all".
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; EMPTY THE HOST NAME BEFORE WE START, and this is not belt and braces.
                ; HNAME is a DS, so it is not in the .COM file at all -- LOAD stops writing
                ; at the last DB, and what is at that address when we run is whatever the
                ; PREVIOUS program left there. Every other buffer here is filled by TOKEN,
                ; which always writes its NUL; HNAME is the one that is only written when
                ; the user gave a second word. Leave it and `W *.HEX` reads the corpse of
                ; the last program's data as a host name and refuses the wildcard.
 03A5 AF        PARSE:  XRA     A
 03A6 327D0B            STA     HNAME
                
 03A9 218000            LXI     H,TAIL
 03AC 46                MOV     B,M             ; length of the tail
 03AD 23                INX     H               ; -> the first character
                
 03AE CD2704            CALL    SKIPSP
 03B1 11BE0B            LXI     D,CNAME
 03B4 0E40              MVI     C,HNMAX
 03B6 CD3304            CALL    TOKEN
                
 03B9 CD2704            CALL    SKIPSP
 03BC 11FF0B            LXI     D,TOK2
 03BF 0E40              MVI     C,HNMAX
 03C1 CD3304            CALL    TOKEN
                
 03C4 CD2704            CALL    SKIPSP
 03C7 11400C            LXI     D,TOK3
 03CA 0E40              MVI     C,HNMAX
 03CC CD3304            CALL    TOKEN
                
 03CF 3A400C            LDA     TOK3
 03D2 B7                ORA     A
 03D3 CAE203            JZ      PA2             ; only two words (or fewer)
                
                ; Three words: the second is the host name and the third is the switch.
 03D6 21FF0B            LXI     H,TOK2
 03D9 CD1B04            CALL    CPYHN
 03DC 21400C            LXI     H,TOK3
 03DF C30704            JMP     SETMOD
                
 03E2 3AFF0B    PA2:    LDA     TOK2
 03E5 B7                ORA     A
 03E6 C8                RZ                      ; one word: nothing more to work out
                
 03E7 3A000C            LDA     TOK2+1
 03EA B7                ORA     A
 03EB C20104            JNZ     PA3             ; longer than one character -> a host name
 03EE 3AFF0B            LDA     TOK2
 03F1 FE42              CPI     'B'
 03F3 CAFB03            JZ      PA2S
 03F6 FE54              CPI     'T'
 03F8 C20104            JNZ     PA3
 03FB 21FF0B    PA2S:   LXI     H,TOK2
 03FE C30704            JMP     SETMOD
                
 0401 21FF0B    PA3:    LXI     H,TOK2
 0404 C31B04            JMP     CPYHN           ; tail call
                
 0407 7E        SETMOD: MOV     A,M
 0408 FE42              CPI     'B'
 040A CA1204            JZ      SM1
 040D FE54              CPI     'T'
 040F C29805            JNZ     EBADSW
 0412 32D805    SM1:    STA     MODE
 0415 3E01              MVI     A,1
 0417 32D905            STA     MODSET          ; a switch was given: PICKMD does not look
 041A C9                RET                     ; at the extension
                
 041B 117D0B    CPYHN:  LXI     D,HNAME
 041E 7E        CH1:    MOV     A,M
 041F 12                STAX    D
 0420 B7                ORA     A
 0421 C8                RZ                      ; the NUL is copied too
 0422 23                INX     H
 0423 13                INX     D
 0424 C31E04            JMP     CH1
                
 0427 78        SKIPSP: MOV     A,B
 0428 B7                ORA     A
 0429 C8                RZ                      ; nothing left
 042A 7E                MOV     A,M
 042B FE20              CPI     ' '
 042D C0                RNZ
 042E 23                INX     H
 042F 05                DCR     B
 0430 C32704            JMP     SKIPSP
                
                ; Copy one space-delimited word from (HL) to (DE), NUL-terminated.
                ;   B = characters left in the tail   C = room at DE, including the NUL
                ; Always writes the NUL -- so "no word here" comes out as an empty string,
                ; which is exactly what the callers test for.
 0433 79        TOKEN:  MOV     A,C
 0434 FE01              CPI     1
 0436 CA4C04            JZ      TOKEND          ; only room for the terminator
 0439 78                MOV     A,B
 043A B7                ORA     A
 043B CA4C04            JZ      TOKEND
 043E 7E                MOV     A,M
 043F FE20              CPI     ' '
 0441 CA4C04            JZ      TOKEND
 0444 12                STAX    D
 0445 23                INX     H
 0446 13                INX     D
 0447 05                DCR     B
 0448 0D                DCR     C
 0449 C33304            JMP     TOKEN
 044C AF        TOKEND: XRA     A
 044D 12                STAX    D
 044E C9                RET
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; TALKING TO THE USER
                ;=======================================================================
                
                ; "W: FOO.ASM -> SRC/FOO.ASM"
 044F 114C09    SAYXFR: LXI     D,MPFX
 0452 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 0454 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 0457 CD0505            CALL    PRFCB
 045A 115009            LXI     D,MARROW
 045D 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 045F CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 0462 217D0B            LXI     H,HNAME
 0465 CDF604            CALL    PSTR
 0468 3A530B            LDA     AUTO
 046B B7                ORA     A
 046C CA7704            JZ      SX1
 046F 115509            LXI     D,MBYEXT        ; " (text, by extension)"
 0472 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 0474 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 0477 118309    SX1:    LXI     D,MCRLF
 047A 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 047C C30500            JMP     BDOS
                
                ; "  12 records, 1493 bytes"
 047F 2A540B    SAYREC: LHLD    RECS
 0482 CD4B05            CALL    PRDEC
 0485 116B09            LXI     D,MRECS         ; " records, "
 0488 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 048A CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 048D CDA904            CALL    PRBYT           ; the exact number of bytes we sent to the host
 0490 117609            LXI     D,MBYTES        ; " bytes",CR,LF
 0493 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 0495 C30500            JMP     BDOS
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; INCBYT -- add one to the 24-bit counter at BYTES. Touches only HL (saved
                ; and restored) and the flags, so A, B and C survive the copy loop.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 0498 E5        INCBYT: PUSH    H
 0499 21560B            LXI     H,BYTES
 049C 34                INR     M
 049D C2A704            JNZ     IB1             ; no carry out of the low byte -- done
 04A0 23                INX     H
 04A1 34                INR     M
 04A2 C2A704            JNZ     IB1
 04A5 23                INX     H
 04A6 34                INR     M
 04A7 E1        IB1:    POP     H
 04A8 C9                RET
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; PRBYT -- print the 24-bit value at BYTES in decimal, the same backwards-
                ; into-the-buffer way PRDEC prints HL. Divides BYTES by ten in place.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 04A9 11AA0C    PRBYT:  LXI     D,NUMEND
 04AC 3E24              MVI     A,'$'
 04AE 12                STAX    D
 04AF 1B        PRB1:   DCX     D
 04B0 D5                PUSH    D               ; DIV10T eats DE
 04B1 CDC904            CALL    DIV10T          ; BYTES = BYTES/10, A = the remainder
 04B4 D1                POP     D
 04B5 C630              ADI     '0'
 04B7 12                STAX    D
 04B8 3A560B            LDA     BYTES           ; anything left?
 04BB 21570B            LXI     H,BYTES+1
 04BE B6                ORA     M
 04BF 23                INX     H
 04C0 B6                ORA     M
 04C1 C2AF04            JNZ     PRB1
 04C4 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 04C6 C30500            JMP     BDOS
                
                ; DIV10T -- BYTES (24-bit) = BYTES/10, remainder returned in A. Long
                ; division from the top byte down: a running remainder r<10 makes
                ; r*256+byte < 2560, which fits in DE, so each byte is one small divide.
 04C9 AF        DIV10T: XRA     A               ; the running remainder starts at 0
 04CA 21580B            LXI     H,BYTES+2       ; most significant byte first
 04CD CDD804            CALL    DT1
 04D0 2B                DCX     H
 04D1 CDD804            CALL    DT1
 04D4 2B                DCX     H
 04D5 C3D804            JMP     DT1             ; tail: A = the final remainder
                
                ; DT1 -- acc = A*256 + (HL); (HL) = acc/10; A = acc MOD 10.  HL is left
                ; where it was, so DIV10T just steps it down a byte between calls.
 04D8 57        DT1:    MOV     D,A             ; DE = acc, D the high byte (< 10)
 04D9 5E                MOV     E,M
 04DA 0600              MVI     B,0             ; the quotient byte
 04DC 7A        DT2:    MOV     A,D
 04DD B7                ORA     A
 04DE C2E704            JNZ     DT3             ; high byte set -> acc >= 256, so >= 10
 04E1 7B                MOV     A,E
 04E2 FE0A              CPI     10
 04E4 DAF304            JC      DT4             ; acc < 10 -> E is the remainder
 04E7 7B        DT3:    MOV     A,E
 04E8 D60A              SUI     10
 04EA 5F                MOV     E,A
 04EB 7A                MOV     A,D
 04EC DE00              SBI     0
 04EE 57                MOV     D,A
 04EF 04                INR     B
 04F0 C3DC04            JMP     DT2
 04F3 70        DT4:    MOV     M,B             ; store the quotient byte back in place
 04F4 7B                MOV     A,E             ; the remainder feeds the next byte down
 04F5 C9                RET
                
                ; PSTR -- print the NUL-terminated string at HL.
                ; HL is pushed around the BDOS call because BDOS EATS EVERY REGISTER.
 04F6 7E        PSTR:   MOV     A,M
 04F7 B7                ORA     A
 04F8 C8                RZ
 04F9 E5                PUSH    H
 04FA 5F                MOV     E,A
 04FB 0E02              MVI     C,CONOUT
 04FD CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 0500 E1                POP     H
 0501 23                INX     H
 0502 C3F604            JMP     PSTR
                
                ; PRFCB -- print the FCB as "d:NAME.EXT".
 0505 3A590B    PRFCB:  LDA     FCB
 0508 B7                ORA     A
 0509 CA1605            JZ      PF1             ; drive 0 = "the current one"; do not print it
 050C C640              ADI     'A'-1
 050E CD4505            CALL    PUTCH
 0511 3E3A              MVI     A,':'
 0513 CD4505            CALL    PUTCH
 0516 215A0B    PF1:    LXI     H,FCB+1
 0519 0608              MVI     B,8
 051B CD3005            CALL    PFLD
 051E 3A620B            LDA     FCB+9
 0521 E67F              ANI     7FH             ; an attribute bit, not a letter -- see FLD
 0523 FE20              CPI     ' '
 0525 C8                RZ                      ; no extension: nothing to print
 0526 3E2E              MVI     A,'.'
 0528 CD4505            CALL    PUTCH
 052B 21620B            LXI     H,FCB+9
 052E 0603              MVI     B,3
 0530 7E        PFLD:   MOV     A,M             ; print B characters at HL, skipping the
 0531 E67F              ANI     7FH             ; (no attribute bits -- see FLD)
 0533 FE20              CPI     ' '             ; blanks an FCB pads with
 0535 CA3F05            JZ      PFL2
 0538 C5                PUSH    B
 0539 E5                PUSH    H
 053A CD4505            CALL    PUTCH
 053D E1                POP     H
 053E C1                POP     B
 053F 23        PFL2:   INX     H
 0540 05                DCR     B
 0541 C23005            JNZ     PFLD
 0544 C9                RET
                
 0545 5F        PUTCH:  MOV     E,A
 0546 0E02              MVI     C,CONOUT
 0548 C30500            JMP     BDOS
                
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
                ; PRDEC -- print HL in decimal.
                ;
                ; Built backwards from the end of a buffer, so no digit reversal is needed,
                ; and terminated with '$' so BDOS 9 prints the whole thing in one call.
                ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 054B 11AA0C    PRDEC:  LXI     D,NUMEND
 054E 3E24              MVI     A,'$'
 0550 12                STAX    D
 0551 1B        PD1:    DCX     D
 0552 CD6205            CALL    DIV10           ; HL = HL/10, A = the remainder
 0555 C630              ADI     '0'
 0557 12                STAX    D
 0558 7C                MOV     A,H
 0559 B5                ORA     L
 055A C25105            JNZ     PD1             ; ...until nothing is left
 055D 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 055F C30500            JMP     BDOS            ; DE already points at the first digit
                
                ; DIV10 -- HL = HL/10, A = the remainder. The 8080 has no divide, so this
                ; is repeated subtraction. A record count is small and this is not a hot
                ; loop.
 0562 C5        DIV10:  PUSH    B
 0563 010000            LXI     B,0
 0566 7C        DV1:    MOV     A,H
 0567 B7                ORA     A
 0568 C27105            JNZ     DV2             ; H nonzero -> definitely >= 10
 056B 7D                MOV     A,L
 056C FE0A              CPI     10
 056E DA7D05            JC      DV3             ; < 10: what is left is the remainder
 0571 7D        DV2:    MOV     A,L
 0572 D60A              SUI     10
 0574 6F                MOV     L,A
 0575 7C                MOV     A,H
 0576 DE00              SBI     0
 0578 67                MOV     H,A
 0579 03                INX     B
 057A C36605            JMP     DV1
 057D 7D        DV3:    MOV     A,L             ; the remainder -- grab it BEFORE clobbering L
 057E 60                MOV     H,B
 057F 69                MOV     L,C             ; HL = the quotient
 0580 C1                POP     B
 0581 C9                RET
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; ERRORS
                ;=======================================================================
                ; THE ONES BELOW THAT WARM-BOOT ARE REACHED FROM `START` OR `MANY`, WHICH
                ; ARE JUMP TARGETS AND NOT SUBROUTINES. The stack is empty there, so a RET
                ; would pop garbage and execute it. ENOCPM is the only one reached from
                ; inside ONEF -- which WAS called -- so it is the only one that may return.
 0582 116006    USAGE:  LXI     D,MUSAGE
 0585 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 0587 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 058A C30000            JMP     0
                
 058D 118609    EWILD:  LXI     D,MEWILD
 0590 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 0592 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 0595 C30000            JMP     0
                
 0598 11F109    EBADSW: LXI     D,MEBADSW
 059B 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 059D CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 05A0 C30000            JMP     0
                
 05A3 11300A    ENOMAT: LXI     D,MENOMAT
 05A6 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 05A8 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 05AB C30000            JMP     0
                
 05AE 114B0A    ETOOMNY:LXI     D,METOOM
 05B1 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 05B3 CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 05B6 C30000            JMP     0
                
                ; Print the message, return with CARRY SET so a wildcard walk can go on to
                ; the next file instead of giving up on all of them.
 05B9 11BA0A    ENOCPM: LXI     D,MENOCPM
 05BC 0E09              MVI     C,PRSTR
 05BE CD0500            CALL    BDOS
 05C1 37                STC
 05C2 C9                RET
                
                ;=======================================================================
                ; DATA
                ;=======================================================================
 05C3 414C544149IDWANT: DB      'ALTAIRSIM HOSTBRIDGE',0
                
 05D8 42        MODE:   DB      'B'             ; the switch, when one was given
 05D9 00        MODSET: DB      0               ; 1 = a switch was given. A DB, not a DS: it
                                                ; must be 0 when we start, and a DS is not
                                                ; in the .COM file at all
                
                ; The extensions that mean TEXT when no switch is given. Three characters
                ; each, and a 0 ends the table. See the top for why it lists text and not
                ; binary, and why .BAS and .DOC are not here.
 05DA 41534D4D41EXTTAB: DB      'ASM','MAC','Z80','HEX','PRN'
 05E9 4C53545359        DB      'LST','SYM','INC','SUB','TXT'
 05F8 00                DB      0
 05F9 00        DRIVE:  DB      0
                
 05FA 0D0A573A20MNOHB:  DB      CR,LF,'W: no host bridge at B0H.',CR,LF
 0617 5468697320        DB      'This machine has no `hostbridge` card in it, or it is at'
 064F 20616E6F74        DB      ' another port.',CR,LF,'$'
                
 0660 0D0A57202DMUSAGE: DB      CR,LF,'W -- copy a file from CP/M out to the host.  (1.2)',CR,LF,CR,LF
 0698 2020205720        DB      '   W <cpmfile> [hostfile] [B|T]',CR,LF
 06B9 2020205720        DB      '   W <pattern> [B|T]',CR,LF,CR,LF
 06D1 4578616D70        DB      'Examples',CR,LF
 06DB 2020205720        DB      '   W FOO.ASM            CP/M FOO.ASM -> host FOO.ASM',CR,LF
 0711 2020205720        DB      '   W FOO.ASM SRC/F.ASM  ...into a subdirectory of hostdir',CR,LF
 074C 2020205720        DB      '   W FOO.DAT T          stop at the first ^Z',CR,LF
 077A 2020205720        DB      '   W *.*                every match, each under its own name'
 07B6 0D0A0D0A          DB      CR,LF,CR,LF
 07BA 4220636F70        DB      'B copies every byte of every record, so a text file arrives'
 07F5 2077697468        DB      ' with',CR,LF
 07FC 7468652072        DB      'the rest of its last record: a ^Z and whatever follows it.'
 0836 0D0A              DB      CR,LF
 0838 542073746F        DB      'T stops at the first ^Z, which TRUNCATES anything binary.'
 0871 0D0A              DB      CR,LF
 0873 5769746820        DB      'With neither, the extension decides. These are text:',CR,LF
 08A9 2020204153        DB      '   ASM MAC Z80 HEX PRN LST SYM INC SUB TXT',CR,LF
 08D5 4576657279        DB      'Every other file, a .COM included, is copied byte for byte.'
 0910 0D0A              DB      CR,LF
 0912 4844495220        DB      'HDIR lists what is on the host. R copies the other way.'
 0949 0D0A24            DB      CR,LF,'$'
                
 094C 573A2024  MPFX:   DB      'W: $'
 0950 202D3E2024MARROW: DB      ' -> $'
 0955 2028746578MBYEXT: DB      ' (text, by extension)$'
 096B 207265636FMRECS:  DB      ' records, $'
 0976 2062797465MBYTES: DB      ' bytes',CR,LF,'$'
 097F 573A2024  MERR:   DB      'W: $'
 0983 0D0A24    MCRLF:  DB      CR,LF,'$'
                
 0986 573A206120MEWILD: DB      'W: a wildcard copies each file under its own name -- so a'
 09BF 207365636F        DB      ' second name',CR,LF,'   cannot mean anything. Drop it.'
 09EE 0D0A24            DB      CR,LF,'$'
 09F1 573A207468MEBADSW:DB      'W: the switch is B (every byte) or T (stop at the first ^Z).'
 0A2D 0D0A24            DB      CR,LF,'$'
 0A30 573A206E6FMENOMAT:DB      'W: no CP/M file matches.',CR,LF,'$'
 0A4B 573A206D6FMETOOM: DB      'W: more than 64 files match. Narrow the pattern.',CR,LF
 0A7D 2020204E6F        DB      '   Nothing was copied -- half a backup is worse than none.'
 0AB7 0D0A24            DB      CR,LF,'$'
 0ABA 573A206E6FMENOCPM:DB      'W: no such CP/M file.',CR,LF,'$'
                
                ;---- Variables -------------------------------------------------------
                ; THE DMA BUFFER -- one CP/M record. BDOS reads each record INTO here (we
                ; point it at this with FSETDMA), and COPY hands it to the card a byte at a
                ; time. BDOS SEARCH also uses it, for the directory sector it found.
 0AD2           BUF:    DS      RECLEN
                
 0B52           XMODE:  DS      1               ; B or T for the file in flight -- see PICKMD
 0B53           AUTO:   DS      1               ; 1 = the extension chose T, so SAYXFR says so
 0B54           RECS:   DS      2               ; records read from CP/M
 0B56           BYTES:  DS      3               ; and the true byte count, 24 bits: even the card's
                                                ; 8 MB ceiling needs more than RECS's 16
 0B59           FCB:    DS      36              ; ours, copied out of the CCP's -- see INIFCB
 0B7D           HNAME:  DS      HNMAX+1         ; the host name (given, or derived)
 0BBE           CNAME:  DS      HNMAX+1         ; the first word of the tail, as typed
 0BFF           TOK2:   DS      HNMAX+1
 0C40           TOK3:   DS      HNMAX+1
 0C81           IDBUF:  DS      IDMAX+1         ; what the card said its name was
 0CA2           NUMBUF: DS      8               ; wide enough for a 24-bit count: 16777215 is 8 digits
 0CAA           NUMEND: DS      1               ; PRDEC and PRBYT build backwards from here
                
 0CAB           NCNT:   DS      1               ; how many wildcard matches we found
 0CAC           DONE:   DS      1               ; how many of them we have copied
 0CAD           NPTR:   DS      2               ; the collect cursor into NAMES
 0CAF           CPTR:   DS      2               ; the copy cursor into NAMES
 0CB1           NAMES:  DS      MAXN*11         ; eleven characters each, no dot, no drive
                
 0F71                   DS      64              ; stack: BDOS is the deepest thing we call
 0FB1 =         STACK   EQU     $
                
 0FB1                   END     START
